    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "must_have",
    "case_for": "Brilliant Corners is securely within the canon’s central post-bebop territory and carries exceptional consensus evidence: the dossier identifies it as a Penguin Guide core selection and DownBeat’s most acclaimed jazz LP of 1957. It is a defining document of Monk’s Riverside resurgence, centered on his singular compositional language in works including \"Brilliant Corners,\" \"Pannonica,\" and \"Bemsha Swing,\" while the sessions join Monk with Sonny Rollins, Max Roach, Ernie Henry, Oscar Pettiford, Clark Terry, and Paul Chambers (S2, S4). The famously difficult, assembled title performance is not merely lore but evidence of Monk extending rhythmic and formal demands on elite peers; the resulting record remains unusually vivid as both an introduction to Monk and a demonstration of his long influence on jazz composition and improvisation.",
    "case_against": "The most substantive reservation is curatorial rather than qualitative: Monk has several plausible defining albums, so a tightly limited canon could favor Monk’s Music, the Coltrane collaboration, or later Monk releases instead of treating this as uniquely indispensable. Its title-track assembly and unevenly constituted sessions can also make it feel less like a naturally unified band statement than some competing Monk albums, and the solo-standard interlude is less central to the album’s thesis. The dossier’s strongest prestige claims rely largely on secondary/reference sources and assert the DownBeat and Penguin judgments without supplying the underlying primary citations.",
